  "metadata": {
    "title": "Apple Bets Big on Siri AI. Investors Aren’t Buying It.",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "THE GIST\nOur analysts just identified a stock with the potential to be the next Nvidia. Tell us how you invest and we'll show you why it's our #1 pick. Tap here.\nApple’s stock slid this week after the company announced its new “Siri AI” at its annual WWDC on Monday. Wall Street’s punishing the consumer tech giant for betting its big moment on “upgrading” a tool, Siri, that many already dislike or simply ignore.\nBut the market's reaction may not be entirely Siri’s fault. It could be optics: Apple put Apple Intelligence front-and-center, trying to appear as a vanguard of the AI revolution without taking on any real risk.\nWHAT HAPPENED\nLong gone are the days of Apple’s infamous “1984” commercial where an unnamed heroine, dressed in what looks like a futuristic Hooters uniform, hurls a sledgehammer at Big Brother.\nSiri AI will be Apple’s new, “profoundly more capable” assistant. The promise is a better-organized life: something that answers a quick question, surfaces a forgotten detail, or pulls up something specific from an earlier chat. Apple’s pitch is a second brain in the cloud, calibrated to the contours of your life — albeit within its own ecosystem — with deeper access to personal context, screen awareness, app actions, conversation history, and cross-device behavior.\nThe company insists Apple Intelligence handles all \"personalization\" locally and runs no training on your private content or interactions, a claim that has invited skepticism.\nApple staged the demo at its Cupertino campus, showing off a cleaner, more responsive Siri handling a chain of everyday multi-step requests. For example, asking about the World Cup, organizing a watch party for a specific game, and then planning country-specific dishes, all in one conversation. Apple then pushed it personal: a demo user asked Siri about something their son had texted them, then told Siri to draft a follow-up email on the subject because, well, time.\nBut neither the market nor Apple’s users were impressed. And many consumers are still upset that Apple’s iPhone 16 Pro never delivered on the company’s lofty promises (see Reddit comments).\nWHY IT MATTERS\nOne stock. Nvidia-level potential. 30M+ investors trust Moby to find it first. Get the pick. Tap here.\nUnlike its competitors in the AI arms race, Apple’s pitch is unique. As Scott Galloway, an NYU marketing professor and podcaster called it: A luxury brand posing as a tech firm, thanks to all the cool, sophisticated urbanites who use Apple products.\nAnd two, Apple has a unique ecosystem that locks users into iPhones, MacBooks, and iPads. Apple has around 2.5 billion active devices, per Statista. Ten years ago, that number was one billion. That’s helped push Apple’s stock north of $317, an all-time-high, though it’s since slipped.",
